{
 "source": "South Burnett -- four predecessor shire IPA Planning Schemes, Schedule 6 \"Non Indigenous Cultural Heritage Sites Listings\" (statutory instruments, gazetted 2006, still in force as the South Burnett Planning Scheme 2017 carries forward pre-amalgamation local heritage listings pending the 2016 Heritage Study register taking full effect)",
 "source_urls": {
  "KINGAROY": "https://www.southburnett.qld.gov.au/files/assets/public/v/1/business/documents/kingaroy_shire_ipa_planning_scheme_schedule_6_heritage_listings.pdf",
  "MURGON": "https://www.southburnett.qld.gov.au/files/assets/public/v/1/business/documents/murgon_shire_ipa_scheme_schedule_6_heritage_listings.pdf",
  "NANANGO": "https://www.southburnett.qld.gov.au/files/assets/public/v/1/business/documents/nanango_shire_ipa_planning_scheme_schedule_6_heritage_listings.pdf",
  "WONDAI": "https://www.southburnett.qld.gov.au/files/assets/public/v/1/business/documents/wondai_shire_ipa_planning_scheme_schedule_6_heritage_listings.pdf"
 },
 "purpose": "South Burnett does not publish a Heritage overlay as a queryable ArcGIS feature service. The council itself links these four Schedule 6 documents from its current Local Heritage Register page (alongside the 2016 Heritage Study final report, which identified 78 places but has no simple machine-readable place/lot-plan table of its own). Schedule 6 is the clean, structured, already-gazetted predecessor to that study -- same role as Livingstone Schedule 3 / Townsville Schedule 7 / Burke Schedule 5.",
 "extraction_method": "PyMuPDF plain-text extraction (p.get_text()), read directly off the page -- automated column-position reconstruction was attempted but proved unreliable (row and wrapped-line spacing are nearly identical, so a Y-gap heuristic cannot distinguish a wrapped cell from the next row). All 88 records were hand-transcribed from the legible plain-text dump by direct reading, consistent with the project convention of visually verifying any extraction that automation cannot be trusted to get right.",
 "cadastre_match_method": "Each record was transcribed with one or more lot/plan candidates (parsed from free-text RPD descriptions like \"Lot 5 on RP36992\" or \"L3/RP103849\"), then queried against the state cadastre (PlanningCadastre/LandParcelPropertyFramework/MapServer/4); the first candidate to resolve to a real parcel is used. 68 of 88 records (77%) resolved. 3 records (war memorials/a well within road reserves) have no lot/plan at all by nature. The remaining 17 unresolved had plausible lot/plan text that did not match any current parcel -- most likely subdivided, renumbered, or superseded since the schedule was gazetted in 2006 (these are mostly 1850s-1920s heritage buildings; a couple of ambiguous single-digit near-misses were found during matching, e.g. a candidate differing by one digit resolved to a real parcel, but these were deliberately NOT substituted in since there is no way to confirm from the source text alone which digit is correct -- only genuine prefix normalisations (e.g. adding an omitted \"RP\") were applied.",
 "caveat": "A hit (within 50m of one of the 68 matched lot/plans) is a reliable positive signal that the property is Schedule 6-listed heritage. A miss is NOT conclusive -- it may be one of the 20 unmatched/road-reserve records. This is a supplementary heads-up, not a substitute for checking the schedules or the councils 2016 Heritage Study directly for a specific address.",
